   "created_utc": "2014-12-12T06:26:58Z",
   "last_edit_utc": "2017-07-14T07:10:41Z",
   "last_activity_utc": "2017-07-14T07:10:41Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "<p>Your best bet is to maintain a table of allocated pointers within your memory management functions. </p>\n\n<p>Don't forget to remove entries when releasing memory.</p>\n"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "comment",
   "post_id": 43316229,
   "parent_id": 27438004,
   "parent_type": "question",
   "author_user_id": 4192931,
   "author_display_name": "JS1",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": null,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/27438004/#comment43316229_27438004",
   "score": 1,
   "created_utc": "2014-12-12T06:27:31Z",
   "edited": false,
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "If you are implementing your own malloc you should be able to keep a list of everything that was allocated, should you choose to do so.  Anything not on that list should be illegal to free."
  },
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 27438073,
   "parent_id": 27438004,
   "author_user_id": 3197055,
   "author_display_name": "Kavindu Dodanduwa",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": false,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/27438073",
   "score": 0,
   "created_utc": "2014-12-12T06:29:10Z",
   "last_edit_utc": null,
   "last_activity_utc": "2014-12-12T06:29:10Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "<p>This might sounds crazy ,</p>\n\n<p>You need to maintain your allocated pointers from your custom malloc() function in a list/structure. Then when using your custom free() function check whether variable exists inside your maintained list/structure. </p>\n"
  },
